# Create JRebirth Maven Project

Scaffold a Maven project that boots JRebirth cleanly, keeps MVC roles separated, and integrates correctly with JPMS.

## Decide the project shape

Before creating any files, choose the right shape:

- **Repository module:** inherit the nearest JRebirth parent POM and use `${project.version}` for JRebirth artifacts. Do not hard-code versions.
- **Standalone app:** use an explicit `jrebirth.version`, add JavaFX dependencies explicitly, configure the Maven compiler plugin, and add the JavaFX Maven plugin.
- **Showcase module:** follow the real examples in `showcase/demo` and `showcase/todos` for the exact structure to copy.

## Standard files

Every new JRebirth module or application includes:

- `pom.xml`
- `src/main/java/module-info.java`
- `src/main/java/<pkg>/<App>.java`
- `src/main/java/<pkg>/ui/MainModel.java`
- `src/main/java/<pkg>/ui/MainView.java`
- `src/main/java/<pkg>/ui/MainController.java`
- Optional: `src/main/java/<pkg>/resources/<App>Styles.java`
- Optional: CSS and resource files under the matching package or resource path.

Templates live in `templates/`.

## Required dependencies

- `org.jrebirth.af:api`
- `org.jrebirth.af:core`
- `org.jrebirth.af:preloader` — only when using JRebirth preloader startup.
- `org.jrebirth.af:processor` — as a provided dependency or annotation processor when `ModuleStarter` generation is needed.
- `javafx.controls` — always required. Add `javafx.fxml`, `javafx.web`, `javafx.media`, or `javafx.swing` only when the application actually uses them.
- A runtime logger such as `ch.qos.logback:logback-classic` for standalone apps.

## JPMS rules

- Use `open module ...` for the normal app or showcase path. It is the simplest approach and matches the showcase modules.
- Export public application, UI, and resource packages as needed.
- Require `org.jrebirth.af.api` and `org.jrebirth.af.core`.
- Add `provides org.jrebirth.af.api.module.ModuleStarter with <pkg>.<Name>ModuleStarter;` for the generated starter. Do not hand-write `<Name>ModuleStarter` — the annotation processor generates it.

## MVC scaffold

- Application extends `DefaultApplication<StackPane>`.
- `firstModelClass()` returns `MainModel.class`.
- Model extends `DefaultModel<MainModel, MainView>` and owns state, bindings, and orchestration.
- View extends `DefaultView<MainModel, BorderPane, MainController>` and builds JavaFX nodes.
- Controller extends `DefaultController<MainModel, MainView>` and wires events to model calls, commands, services, or waves.

## Checks before finishing

- Keep business logic out of the View and Controller.
- Do not use raw threads or `Platform.runLater` as architecture.
- Keep the package names, module name, resource base path, and generated starter name consistent with each other.
- In this repository, verify the build with `mvn clean install -Dmaven.test.skip=true`.
- For a standalone generated project, verify with `mvn clean package`.
- Launch a standalone app with `mvn javafx:run`.
- Launch a repository module with `mvn javafx:run -pl <module-path> -Djavafx.mainClass=<module>/<appClass>`.
- After creating an application, always tell the user the exact build command and the exact launch command for the generated project, with the correct module name and class filled in.

Use `reference.md` for project variants and `templates/` for scaffold files.
